WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.980
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Whether
you are debugging

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:02.880
a specific application
or monitoring

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.520
multiple applications across
several production clusters,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.758
you need logs.

00:00:06.758 --> 00:00:09.300
I'm here today to show you how
to use Cloud Logging to easily

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.860
find and analyze
the logs you need

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.170
from your applications
running on GKE.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.580
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.845
Cloud Logging is deeply
integrated into GKE.

00:00:23.845 --> 00:00:26.220
That means that collecting
infrastructure and application

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.300
logs is easy.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.460
GKE deploys a
per-node logging agent

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.460
that automatically collects
standard output and error logs.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.070
It then sends logs to the
logs router, which sends them

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.620
to Cloud Logging and any of
the logging sink destinations

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:38.910
that you have configured.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.493
You can start viewing your logs
as soon as your application is

00:00:41.493 --> 00:00:43.330
deployed.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.100
The easiest place
to find the logs

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.110
is in the Logs Viewer
in the Cloud Console.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.610
You can get there by selecting
that option from the products

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:50.850
menu.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.450
Another way to access logs just
for your service quickly is

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:54.700
from the GKE Workloads screen.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.510
Just select your deployment
and then click Container Logs.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.790
Either option brings
you to the Logs Viewer.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:03.100
Note that we're looking at
the Logs Viewer Preview.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.600
You may be more familiar with
the classic Logs Viewer, which

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:06.780
has less functionality.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.310
The new Logs Viewer
has four main areas--

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:12.820
the query builder at the top,
the histogram below that,

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.190
the logs field explorer
on the left, and the query

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:16.740
results on the right.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.920
The query builder is at
the top of the screen.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:20.530
This is how you
create and run queries

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.210
to retrieve the
logs that you want.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.670
You can also add query
parameters directly

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.430
and use the autocomplete
to select available fields.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.320
The logs field
explorer on the left

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.660
is there so that you can
easily find logs by the field

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.220
and value in the log payload.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.220
One example would be to look at
all the pods in the namespace.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.920
Since I'm looking at logs for
a specific service already,

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.810
I can click on Error
in the Severity field

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.070
to find just the error
logs for that service.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:44.110
Let's do this now.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:45.730
The histogram at the
top of the screen

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.530
shows the frequency
and relative volume

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:48.820
of the errors you're seeing.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.070
You can also use the histogram
to select specific time

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.660
ranges to search for.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.030
The query results show
the actual log entries

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:56.300
that match our filters.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.300
You can expand any of the
entries in the results

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.770
to see the full log payload.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.970
I'm seeing the authentication
error in my logs.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.270
Finally, you can click on
any of the payload fields

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.043
and select Show Matching Entries
to see all log entries that

00:02:07.043 --> 00:02:09.460
contain this message if you
want to see where else you may

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.200
be getting a particular error.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.450
You've now learned how logs
are ingested from your GKE

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.490
workloads into Cloud
Logging and how

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:18.410
to use the Logs Viewer
and its field explorer

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.540
and histogram features to
find exactly what you're

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:21.282
looking for.

00:02:21.282 --> 00:02:23.240
I hope this helps you to
debug and troubleshoot

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.867
your own workloads on GKE.

00:02:24.867 --> 00:02:25.700
Thanks for watching.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.155
And come back soon for more.

00:02:27.155 --> 00:02:28.280
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.520
Stay healthy out there.

